# Reading Data: Averages, Spread, and Relationships

> Data answer questions only when you summarise them honestly. Three
> averages, two measures of spread, and one golden warning —
> correlation is not causation — carry most of that work.

## 1. Types of Data and Frequency Tables

**Qualitative** data record categories (blood group, favourite
subject); **quantitative** data record amounts, and split into
**discrete** (countable: number of goals) and **continuous**
(measured: height, time). Continuous data are grouped into **class
intervals** (e.g. 150–160, 160–170) with tally marks building a
frequency table.

[definition]
A **frequency table** pairs each value (or class) with how often it
occurs. The total of the frequency column is n, the size of the
dataset.
[/definition]

## 2. Mean, Median, Mode

Marks scored by 20 students: 4 appears 3 times, 6 appears 5 times, 8
appears 8 times, 10 appears 4 times.

@chart bar title="Marks scored by 20 students"
labels:
4 marks
6 marks
8 marks
10 marks

data:
3
5
8
4
@/chart

@math label="eq:mean-frequency"
\bar{x} = \frac{\sum f x}{\sum f} = \frac{3(4)+5(6)+8(8)+4(10)}{20} = \frac{146}{20} = 7.3
@/math

The **median** needs the data in order: with n = 20, average the
10th and 11th values. Cumulative frequencies 3, 8, 16, 20 place both
at 8 marks. The **mode** is the most frequent value: 8.

| Average | Use it when |
| --- | --- |
| Mean | data are fairly even; every value matters |
| Median | skewed data or outliers (income, house prices) |
| Mode | categories ("most popular" transport or snack) |

## 3. Outliers Bend the Mean

One billionaire joining a village of 100 farmers drags the mean
income far above what anyone earns — but the **median** barely moves,
because it counts position, not size. This robustness is why median
income is reported, not mean. Trimmed means discard the top and
bottom few percent for the same reason.

## 4. Range, Quartiles, IQR

- **Range** = maximum − minimum (simple, but outlier-sensitive).
- **Quartiles** split ordered data into four parts: Q1 (25% below),
  Q2 (the median), Q3 (75% below).
- **IQR** = Q3 − Q1, the width of the middle half — robust to
  outliers.

For 3, 5, 6, 7, 9, 10, 12: Q1 = 5, median = 7, Q3 = 10, so
IQR = 5 and range = 9. A **box plot** draws exactly these five
numbers (min, Q1, median, Q3, max): the box spans the IQR, whiskers
the range, and outliers appear as lone dots.

## 5. Spread: Variance and Standard Deviation

**Variance** averages the **squared** deviations from the mean;
**standard deviation** (SD) is its square root, back in the units of
the data.

[example]
Compare A: 4, 5, 6, 7, 8 with B: 1, 3, 6, 9, 11. Both have mean 6,
but A's deviations (−2, −1, 0, 1, 2) give SD ≈ 1.41, while B's
deviations (−5, −3, 0, 3, 5) give SD ≈ 3.69. Same centre, very
different spread — B's SD flags it, the mean cannot.
[/example]

## 6. Reading Charts Honestly

- **Bar chart** — discrete categories with gaps; **histogram** —
  continuous classes with no gaps, area carries frequency.
- **Pie chart** — parts of one whole: angle = fraction × 360°.
- **Line graph** — time series; slope shows the rate of change.

[example]
Transport survey of 30 students: walk 12, bus 10, cycle 5, car 3.
Pie angles: 12/30 × 360° = 144°, 10/30 × 360° = 120°, 5/30 × 360° =
60°, 3/30 × 360° = 36°. The angles must total 360° — a built-in check.
[/example]

[warning]
Misleading charts: a truncated vertical axis magnifies tiny
differences, and a cherry-picked time window hides the trend on
either side. Always check the axis labels and where the axis starts.
[/warning]

## 7. Correlation: Direction, Not Cause

A **scatter plot** of two variables shows **correlation**: points
drifting upward together are **positive** (height and weight),
downward together **negative** (practice hours and typing errors),
a shapeless cloud **none** (shoe size and salary). Tightness of the
cloud measures strength.

[important title="Correlation is not causation"]
Ice-cream sales and drownings rise together every summer — not
because dessert drowns people, but because hot weather drives both.
A **confounding variable** can create a correlation with no causal
link at all. Only controlled experiments can establish causation.
[/important]

A **regression line** is the straight line of best fit through the
cloud; its slope predicts how much the response changes per unit of
the explanatory variable, and predictions work best inside the
observed range (interpolation, not extrapolation).

## 8. Quick Revision

| Question | Answer |
| --- | --- |
| Mean of the 20-student data? | 7.3 |
| Its median and mode? | 8 and 8 |
| Best average for incomes? | median — outliers bend the mean |
| Q1 = 5, Q3 = 10 — IQR? | 5 |
| A: SD 1.41 vs B: SD 3.69 — same mean. Which spread is wider? | B |
| Pie angle for 12 of 30? | 144° |
| Correlation proves causation? | never — look for confounders |
